#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179524 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179669 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5ce5418-6b75-5efa-b7f9-99ee16c510be" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Line Integral of a Vector-Valued Function Along a Union of Curves"}] :question/id #uuid "734d74ca-26cf-57d3-b3dc-fec66d7e0264" :question/math-academy-id "e-6286" :question/problem "![](images/00/001487e4a94988a24d15702a18692e9deb2495a7cc7ec5c9f71b98fea13e0ce8.png)\n\nThe path $C_1$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(-2,0)$ to the point $O(0,0),$ and the path $C_2$ consists of a line segment from the point $O(0,0)$ to the point $B(1,1).$\n\nGiven that $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r} = -4,$ evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r},$ where $\\mathbf{F}(x, y) = (2x-3y) \\,\\mathbf{i} + (2y-5x)\\,\\mathbf {j}$ and $C = C_1\\cup C_2.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the fact that\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r} = \\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r}\n+\n \\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r}.\n$$\n\nWe're given the value of the first integral, so we only need to work out the second.\n\nThe path $C_2$ is the line segment from $O$ to $B.$ It can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{2}(t) & =b+t(c-b) \\\\  & =⟨0,0⟩+t(⟨1,1⟩-⟨0,0⟩) \\\\  & =⟨t,t⟩ \\\\  & =t\\,i+t\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0 \\lt t \\lt 1.$ Along this segment, we have $x = t$ and $y = t.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{2}(t)) & =(2x-3y)\\,i+(2y-5x)\\,j \\\\  & =(2t-3t)\\,i+(2t-5t)\\,j \\\\  & =-t\\,i-3t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{2}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now compute the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{2}(t))\\cdot {r}_{2}^{′}(t) & =(-t\\,i-3t\\,j)\\cdot (i+j) \\\\  & =-t\\cdot 1+(-3t)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-4t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =-4+{\\int }_{0}^{1}F({r}_{2}(t))\\cdot {r}_{2}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4+{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-4t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4+[-2{t}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-4+[-2+0] \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179527 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179672 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "70b4357f-d5c5-5946-9022-0602c4217a1d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Properties of Line Integrals of Vector-Valued Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "2e6103cc-7be6-5eb6-ab5a-aa5aff3fa423" :question/math-academy-id "e-6319" :question/problem "![](images/38/38ab4af726729b5c9804f4aa13801d637e5e62eda085d6611174586827ef8c11.png)\n\nConsider the integral $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r},$ where $C$ is the closed polygonal path $FGHF$ shown above, traversed in the **clockwise** direction. Express this integral as a sum of line integrals along $C_1, C_2,$ and $C_3.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can represent $C,$ which is traversed in clockwise, as the union of $3$ paths:\n\n$$\nC = \\{-C_1\\} \\cup \\{-C_2\\} \\cup C_3,\n$$\n\nwhere $-C_1$ and $-C_2$ are the same paths as $C_1$ and $C_2$ but traversed in the opposite direction.\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179600 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179738 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bed301c-59ac-5cb3-85e4-4aeaeebc15c2" :knowledge-point/title "Establishing the Connection With Line Integrals of Scalar Fields"}] :question/id #uuid "171fef73-d124-5aca-ad06-9f20ae5a087c" :question/math-academy-id "e-9535" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = (3x+2) \\: \\mathbf{i} - y^3 \\: \\mathbf{j}$ and let $C$ be a path in $\\mathbb R^2.$ Write $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}$ in the form $\\displaystyle\\int_C P(x,y)\\,\\textrm d x +  Q(x,y)\\,\\textrm d y.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a piecewise-smooth path $C$ and a vector field \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = P(x,y) \\: \\mathbf{i} + Q(x,y) \\: \\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\ndefined on $C,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} = \\int\\limits_C P(x,y) \\: \\text{d}x + Q(x,y) \\: \\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $P = 3x+2$ and $Q = -y^3.$ Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}(3x+2)\\,\\text{d}x-{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536874 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179738 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bed301c-59ac-5cb3-85e4-4aeaeebc15c2" :knowledge-point/title "Establishing the Connection With Line Integrals of Scalar Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0abe0b0c-c7b6-54cc-89f5-b4e8765eb5d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}(yz+xz)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+xy\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536304} {:answer/id #uuid "9d2c9102-5369-5ba0-ac8a-916120320f57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}yz\\,\\text{d}x+(xz+xy)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536537} {:answer/id #uuid "ae0c8d28-d367-5e4a-9fc3-ee267494558a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}x\\,\\text{d}x+y\\,\\text{d}y+z\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536563} {:answer/id #uuid "c30515bc-7147-5e35-a4a6-aa22b4d891de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}yz\\,\\text{d}x+xz\\,\\text{d}y+xy\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536592} {:answer/id #uuid "e80d1d5b-0eef-53d7-9609-23206eeed2a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}(yz+xy+xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536646}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c30515bc-7147-5e35-a4a6-aa22b4d891de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}yz\\,\\text{d}x+xz\\,\\text{d}y+xy\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536592} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f8c40bf-0ce0-5cf0-89dd-16aa652b7225" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536742}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b7927a26-b99a-5854-aa75-916116f0bbc7" :question/math-academy-id "q-125411" :question/problem "Let $F(x,y,z)=yz\\,i+xz\\,j+xy\\,k$ and let $C$ be a path in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a piecewise-smooth path $C$ and a vector field \n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=P(x,y,z)\\,i+Q(x,y,z)\\,j+R(x,y,z)\\,k\n$$\n\ndefined on $C,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{C}P(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y+R(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}yz\\,\\text{d}x+xz\\,\\text{d}y+xy\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259805 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179738 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bed301c-59ac-5cb3-85e4-4aeaeebc15c2" :knowledge-point/title "Establishing the Connection With Line Integrals of Scalar Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19e81440-e84d-56cd-bb58-ec161c442251" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}x\\,\\text{d}x+({y}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257003} {:answer/id #uuid "5f494e8c-ad73-595a-bf25-8c0366227915" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x+y\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257618} {:answer/id #uuid "7b07b399-bcf2-5d7e-8ea4-1477d0096940" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}({y}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x+x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257873} {:answer/id #uuid "8f63ee0f-d6d5-55d0-a96d-faabbd6baae3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}(x-3y)\\,\\text{d}x+2\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258042} {:answer/id #uuid "fba49b65-428e-5726-bf94-6db8e1ed2b78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}2\\,\\text{d}x+(x-3y)\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258982}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f63ee0f-d6d5-55d0-a96d-faabbd6baae3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}(x-3y)\\,\\text{d}x+2\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258042} :answer-field/id #uuid "021dcd34-65cf-5872-b83c-9f85620594f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259035}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ab23b1e6-350e-57c6-bc34-78c226b2bb0d" :question/math-academy-id "q-125416" :question/problem "Let $F(x,y)=(x-3y)\\,i+2\\,j$ and let $C$ be the path defined by $r(t)=⟨t,\\,{t}^{2}+1⟩$ for $t\\in [0,1].$ Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a piecewise-smooth path $C$ and a vector field \n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j\n$$\n\ndefined on $C,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{C}P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}(x-3y)\\,\\text{d}x+2\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259806 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179738 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bed301c-59ac-5cb3-85e4-4aeaeebc15c2" :knowledge-point/title "Establishing the Connection With Line Integrals of Scalar Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5eb6d40c-1738-5868-a121-ed6bf6c0d399" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}(2y-3x)\\,\\text{d}x-4\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257614} {:answer/id #uuid "751bbc81-29f2-5e3a-8e2b-798df9ec806c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}x\\,\\text{d}x+(3y-1)\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257809} {:answer/id #uuid "88ae962d-8869-54c0-82fc-70c5851fef4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}(3x-1)\\,\\text{d}x+y\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257980} {:answer/id #uuid "bcb0d958-9542-52b7-aab9-12ab08f18e7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}(3y-1)\\,\\text{d}x+x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258428} {:answer/id #uuid "e7c032c0-a028-5aad-8373-05466f052f8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}4\\,\\text{d}x-(2y-3x)\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258789}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7c032c0-a028-5aad-8373-05466f052f8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}4\\,\\text{d}x-(2y-3x)\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258789} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f8a6dd0-aae9-5825-810c-1c9c92473b84" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259146}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0bb48414-d307-548b-9146-c35b5549d965" :question/math-academy-id "q-125417" :question/problem "Let $F(x,y)=4\\,i-(2y-3x)\\,j$ and let $C$ be the path defined by $r(t)=⟨t,\\,3t-1⟩$ for $t\\in [0,1].$ Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a piecewise-smooth path $C$ and a vector field \n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j\n$$\n\ndefined on $C,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{C}P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}4\\,\\text{d}x-(2y-3x)\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415775 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179738 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bed301c-59ac-5cb3-85e4-4aeaeebc15c2" :knowledge-point/title "Establishing the Connection With Line Integrals of Scalar Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3124c0f4-4c6e-55ec-9349-8032b33b382f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{e}^{y-1}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415737} {:answer/id #uuid "990826b9-b6bc-588a-80e6-36af02d8f2ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}{e}^{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x+{e}^{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415750} {:answer/id #uuid "b1e72ea2-4855-5111-a3dd-cd04fb8b2403" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415752} {:answer/id #uuid "e80ae482-54b0-51f8-b405-74f535b48c44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}{e}^{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{e}^{x-1}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415759} {:answer/id #uuid "f546e017-61e1-549c-83d5-ab9a05185633" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}x+{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415762}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f546e017-61e1-549c-83d5-ab9a05185633" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}x+{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415762} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a4c61a4-7435-599c-afcf-0efe6b1cbdb6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415765}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a47f5f1d-1328-52e8-8c90-bfca32946e28" :question/math-academy-id "q-125419" :question/problem "Let $F(x,y)={e}^{y}\\,i+{e}^{x}\\,j$ and let $C$ be the path defined by $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2},\\,t-1⟩$ for $t\\in [0,1].$ Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a piecewise-smooth path $C$ and a vector field \n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j\n$$\n\ndefined on $C,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{C}P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}x+{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180025 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179738 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bed301c-59ac-5cb3-85e4-4aeaeebc15c2" :knowledge-point/title "Establishing the Connection With Line Integrals of Scalar Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0150bf9f-bc52-5d7c-baa1-278be0ec41d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}∇(x+y+z)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536288} {:answer/id #uuid "754a17e4-04df-525d-9c6f-927705d19c10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{z}^{2}\\,i+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536478} {:answer/id #uuid "e4847fce-4e49-589a-b6ee-7837047bacf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z\\,i+x\\,j+y\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536640} {:answer/id #uuid "f52c058f-37bd-5f7c-a31f-2c35690a82a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∇(x+y+z)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536676} {:answer/id #uuid "fe7ad197-686c-54ac-8451-0dbb07ea69a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536691}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4847fce-4e49-589a-b6ee-7837047bacf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z\\,i+x\\,j+y\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536640} :answer-field/id #uuid "215217ab-57d4-55ca-a3b0-79e58a5c7e34" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177819}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f08d86dc-4f87-54db-ae1a-88ae0082bc5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-125422" :question/problem "If ${\\int }_{C}z\\,\\text{d}x+x\\,\\text{d}y+y\\,\\text{d}z$ is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ for some piecewise smooth curve $C,$ then $F(x,y,z)=$" :question/worked-solution "Given a piecewise-smooth path $C$ and a vector field \n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=P(x,y,z)\\,i+Q(x,y,z)\\,j+R(x,y,z)\\,k\n$$\n\ndefined on $C,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y+R\\,\\text{d}z\n$$\n\nIn our case, $P=z,Q=x,R=y.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(z\\,i+x\\,j+y\\,k)\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}z\\,\\text{d}x+x\\,\\text{d}y+y\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $F=z\\,i+x\\,j+y\\,k.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180173 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179669 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5ce5418-6b75-5efa-b7f9-99ee16c510be" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Line Integral of a Vector-Valued Function Along a Union of Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06952f7f-4bfc-548a-858e-98687ea45c52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324032} {:answer/id #uuid "5f81d6d6-6127-530e-ac7f-c4e213abe7ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324769} {:answer/id #uuid "a2f6ee80-a912-57f5-a285-2e0f6930700b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325354} {:answer/id #uuid "aa85942c-1e5b-56e8-8e97-ecf540eb889c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325422} {:answer/id #uuid "b7a60610-258b-5f05-a158-e2b14597409d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325547}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f81d6d6-6127-530e-ac7f-c4e213abe7ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324769} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a311ac2-5c19-587c-a392-8c71de68fc0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326439}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eca1e45a-1d3f-5ae4-a5ee-83a793b7f6b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-39342" :question/problem "![](images/0e/0e4a78829ce8d1135d46dda1db59c968c0306937d9c5a45b24fa41648a679ea4.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a line segment from the point $O(0,0)$ to the point $A(2,0),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(2,0)$ to the point $B(1,1).$\n\nGiven that ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2,$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=(x+y)\\,i+(x-y)\\,j,$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the fact that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$\n\nWe're given the value of the first integral, so we only need to work out the second.\n\nThe path ${C}_{2}$ is the line segment from $A$ to $B.$ It can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{2}(t) & =a+t(b-a) \\\\  & =⟨2,0⟩+t(⟨1,1⟩-⟨2,0⟩) \\\\  & =⟨2-t,t⟩ \\\\  & =(2-t)\\,i+t\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0<t<1.$ Along this segment, we have $x=2-t$ and $y=t.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{2}(t)) & =(x+y)\\,i+(x-y)\\,j \\\\  & =(2-t+t)\\,i+(2-t-t)\\,j \\\\  & =2\\,i+(2-2t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{2}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =-i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now compute the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{2}(t))\\cdot {r}_{2}^{′}(t) & =(2\\,i+(2-2t)\\,j)\\cdot (-i+j) \\\\  & =-2+(2-2t) \\\\  & =-2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =2+{\\int }_{0}^{1}F({r}_{2}(t))\\cdot {r}_{2}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2+{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2-{t}^{2}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2-(1-0) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180175 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179669 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5ce5418-6b75-5efa-b7f9-99ee16c510be" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Line Integral of a Vector-Valued Function Along a Union of Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ad37157-9a52-53de-8a88-f35bbd20faae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324312} {:answer/id #uuid "41eaf9b6-c62c-5a18-b922-6e8c121cab26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324503} {:answer/id #uuid "66de0861-eaa6-53b8-b659-704734a18a2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324831} {:answer/id #uuid "8772142e-f3d2-5968-9d7e-fa44127516d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325124} {:answer/id #uuid "bc84b5b2-8cab-547c-8cee-e174ba0ddddd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325588}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41eaf9b6-c62c-5a18-b922-6e8c121cab26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324503} :answer-field/id #uuid "813be4d0-fa70-524e-afa9-65eb377f0d76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326454}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3e395277-17c1-5003-9ac0-10817fce6c94" :question/math-academy-id "q-39674" :question/problem "![](images/51/512905447203d1d3a96781d20d586f9c4cf5cc9392fab50607b6fc77e9b00121.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a section of the parabola $y={x}^{2}$ from the point $O(0,0)$ to the point $A(2,4),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(2,4)$ to the point $B(0,4).$\n\nGiven that ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-2,$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=x\\,i+y\\,j$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the fact that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$\n\nWe're given the value of the second integral, so we only need to work out the first.\n\n${C}_{1}$ is the section of parabola $y={x}^{2}$ from $O$ to $A.$ It can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{1}(t)=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0<t<2.$ Along this curve, we have $x=t$ and $y={t}^{2}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{1}(t)) & =x\\,i+y\\,j \\\\  & =t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{1}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})j \\\\  & =i+2t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now compute the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{1}(t))\\cdot {r}_{1}^{′}(t) & =(t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j)\\cdot (i+2t\\,j) \\\\  & =t+2{t}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}F({r}_{1}(t))\\cdot {r}_{1}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t-2 \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(t+2{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t-2 \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{t}^{4}}{2}]}_{0}^{2}-2 \\\\  & =[2+8]-2 \\\\  & =10-2 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180279 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179672 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "70b4357f-d5c5-5946-9022-0602c4217a1d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Properties of Line Integrals of Vector-Valued Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "092e02f4-a48a-5e04-9e64-41122eafc4c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342314} {:answer/id #uuid "32123023-9c69-58b4-ab02-b4da1d9fe637" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342661} {:answer/id #uuid "3ee53d4c-ce0e-5c65-b50c-2b82aac4e434" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342759} {:answer/id #uuid "7d3356df-04ac-5b19-8ede-9a2b3f13406b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343316} {:answer/id #uuid "d7fb15c7-bb0e-5a45-9c69-b48603c7d63d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344137}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d3356df-04ac-5b19-8ede-9a2b3f13406b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343316} :answer-field/id #uuid "75ad2dbc-8ee7-590b-b513-269ed66bd87a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344704}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9aa3be4f-70c9-57d5-8b81-21b143de9479" :question/math-academy-id "q-76473" :question/problem "![](images/10/103c586778cdd8181fdc8396a92472df0525fc8e1c77fde9a7c3f731dca6df07.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is the closed polygonal path $OABO,$ shown in the diagram above, traversed in the counterclockwise direction?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can represent $C,$ which is traversed in counterclockwise, as the union of $3$ paths:\n\n$$\nC={C}_{1}\\cup \\{-{C}_{2}\\}\\cup {C}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $-{C}_{2}$ is the same path as ${C}_{2}$ but traversed in the opposite direction.\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180280 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179672 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "70b4357f-d5c5-5946-9022-0602c4217a1d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Properties of Line Integrals of Vector-Valued Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1409abbb-7224-58eb-8da4-c50ec502212f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\tex